nFor $k=3$, $n = 8(3)+6 = 30$.\nFor $k=4$, $n = 8(4)+6 = 38$.\nFor $k=5$, $n = 8(5)+6 = 46$.\nFor $k=6$, $n = 8(6)+6 = 54$.\nLet's check these values.\nIf $k=2$, $n=22, b=3(2)+2=8, r=5(2)+4=14$. $r \\ge 7, b \\ge 7$ are satisfied.\nIf $k=3$, $n=30, b=3(3)+2=11, r=5(3)+4=19$. $r \\ge 7, b \\ge 7$ are satisfied.\nIf $k=4$, $n=38, b=3(4)+2=14, r=5(4)+4=24$. $r \\ge 7, b \\ge 7$ are satisfied.\nIf $k=5$, $n=46, b=3(5)+2=17, r=5(5)+4=29$. $r \\ge 7, b \\ge 7$ are satisfied.\nIf $k=6$, $n=54, b=3(6)+2=20, r=5(6)+4=34$. $r \\ge 7, b \\ge 7$ are satisfied.\nThe sum of these five least values of $n$ is:\n$22 + 30 + 38 + 46 + 54 = (22+54) + (30+46) + 38 = 76 + 76 + 38 = 152 + 38 = 190$.<channel|>To solve the problem, let $n$ be the total number of marbles, $r$ be the number of red marbles, and $b$ be the number of blue marbles. We are given $r+b=n$, $r \\ge 7$, and $b \\ge 7$. When 7 marbles are drawn without replacement, the probability that exactly $k$ of them are red is given by the hypergeometric distribution:\n$$P(X=k) = \\frac{\\binom{r}{k} \\binom{b}{7-k}}{\\binom{n}{7}}$$\nWe are given that $P(X=4) = P(X=5)$, which implies:\n$$\\frac{\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3}}{\\binom{n}{7}} = \\frac{\\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}}{\\binom{n}{7}}$$\n$$\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3} = \\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}$$\nExpanding the combinations:\n$$\\frac{r!}{4!(r-4)!} \\frac{b!}{3!(b-3)!} = \\frac{r!}{5!(r-5)!} \\frac{b!}{2!(b-2)!}$$\nDividing both sides by $r!b!$ and simplifying:\n$$\\frac{1}{4!(r-4)!} \\frac{1}{3!(b-3)!} = \\frac{1}{5!(r-5)!} \\frac{1}{2!(b-2)!}$$\nMultiplying both by $5!$ and $3!$:\n$$\\frac{5!}{4!} \\frac{(r-5)!}{(r-4)!} = \\frac{3!}{2!} \\frac{(b-3)!}{(b-2)!}$$\n$$5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{r-4} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{b-2}$$\n$$5(b-2) = 3(r-4) \\implies 5b - 10 = 3r - 12 \\implies 3r = 5b + 2$$\nSubstituting $r = n-b$:\n$$3(n-b) = 5b + 2 \\implies 3n - 3b = 5b + 2 \\implies 8b = 3n - 2 \\implies b = \\frac{3n-2}{8}$$\nFor $b$ to be an integer, $3n-2$ must be divisible by 8:\n$$3n